  (93, 'Cramp Bark', 'Viburnum opulus', 'Bark', 'Viopudial', 'Terpenoid', 'Sesquiterpene', 'High', 'Marker', 'Principal smooth muscle antispasmodic constituent contributing to relief of uterine and gastrointestinal cramping.', 'This entry nicely validates one of the goals of your database. Cramp Bark and Black Haw emerge as chemically related herbs—not because they share a common name, but because they genuinely share scopoletin, esculetin, chlorogenic acid, and related bark chemistry. The distinguishing constituent is viopudial, which appears unique to Cramp Bark and likely explains why it is generally regarded as the stronger smooth muscle antispasmodic of the two.'),
  (93, 'Cramp Bark', 'Viburnum opulus', 'Bark', 'Scopoletin', 'Coumarin', 'Simple coumarin', 'High', 'Major', 'Contributes antispasmodic and mild anti-inflammatory activity on smooth muscle.', NULL),
